<Window xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        x:Class="ClaudeBuddy.OrbFlyout"
        Width="26" Height="26"
        SizeToContent="Manual"
        SystemDecorations="None"
        TransparencyLevelHint="Transparent"
        Background="Transparent"
        Topmost="True"
        ShowInTaskbar="False"
        CanResize="False"
        ShowActivated="False"
        WindowStartupLocation="Manual">

    <!-- One action today (voice dictation); a home for more later, stacked
         further from the orb as they're added — which is the reason this is
         its own window positioned next to the orb rather than a control
         drawn inside OrbWindow's own 56x56 bounds. That size is load-bearing
         elsewhere (drag hit-testing, SessionManager's stacking/reflow,
         TeamLinks' arrow anchor all assume it), so a second action would
         have had nowhere to go without pulling this out.

         A tether line to the orb, and the button itself smaller than the
         orb (24px here vs. the orb's own 36px) — both make this read as
         "belongs to that orb" rather than as an unrelated floating button
         nearby, the way the connecting arrows already do for team members
         in TeamLinks. Simpler than TeamLinks' own arrows on purpose: this
         window's position is always the same fixed offset from its orb (see
         OrbWindow.MicFlyoutOffset), never two independently-dragged points,
         so the line's start and end are constants rather than geometry
         recomputed from wherever two orbs currently happen to be.

         Root is pinned to 26x26 and anchored top-left because this window
         does not get to be 26x26 on Windows: the OS floors a top-level
         window at SM_CXMINTRACK/SM_CYMINTRACK (136x39 on a stock Windows 11
         desktop), which arrives here as a 120x39 client area. Measured on a
         real machine, and not negotiable — Width/Height, MinWidth/MaxWidth,
         and a direct SetWindowPos from outside the process all leave it at
         the floor. macOS honours the 26x26, which is why every symptom of
         this was Windows-only.

         Anchoring at the top-left is what makes that harmless: the corner is
         the one point of this window whose screen location the app actually
         controls (OrbWindow sets Position to the orb's own circle edge), so
         everything below is placed relative to it and the leftover window
         area is just transparent slack off to the right. Anything measured
         from the far edges instead lands wherever the floor happens to put
         them — the mic button, bottom-right-aligned inside a client area
         four times wider than asked for, sat ~95px right and ~13px below the
         end of its own tether line, which is the whole bug this comment
         replaces two wrong explanations of. -->
    <Grid x:Name="Root"
          Width="26" Height="26"
          HorizontalAlignment="Left" VerticalAlignment="Top">
        <!-- EndPoint touches the button's actual circle, not its 24x24
             bounding box: that box spans (2,2)-(26,26) (bottom-right-aligned
             in Root's 26x26 — not the window's own size, see above, which is
             exactly the distinction this got wrong), so its centre is
             (14,14) with a 12px
             radius, and the circle's true edge toward the line sits inset
             from the box corner at roughly 14 - 12*cos(45°) ≈ 5.7 — the same
             inscribed-circle gap corrected for on the orb end of this line
             (see MicFlyoutOffset in OrbWindow), just showing up at the other
             end instead. -->
        <Line StartPoint="0,0" EndPoint="5.7,5.7"
              Stroke="#80FFFFFF" StrokeThickness="1.5"
              IsHitTestVisible="False"/>

        <Grid x:Name="MicButton"
              Width="24" Height="24"
              HorizontalAlignment="Right"
              VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
              Cursor="Hand">
            <Ellipse Fill="#E0202024" Stroke="#80FFFFFF" StrokeThickness="1.5"/>
            <!-- 11, not 13. The glyph is a diagonal shape, so its corner-to-corner
                 extent is what has to fit, not its nominal size: at 13 the mic's
                 head sat on the ring's inner edge with the handle reaching the
                 opposite one, which read as an icon slightly too big for the
                 button it's in. 11 leaves visible clearance on both ends while
                 still filling the circle. -->
            <TextBlock Text="&#127908;"
                       FontSize="11"
                       HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center"
                       IsHitTestVisible="False"/>
        </Grid>
    </Grid>
</Window>
